    "seconds": 81,
    "summary": "The HighRequestErrorRate alert is mitigated. The frontend was failing on DNS, and I restored its DNS setting.\n\n**Cause:** About 4 minutes before I looked, the `frontend` deployment rolled out a new revision (2). That revision changed the pod's `dnsPolicy` from `ClusterFirst` to `Default`. With `Default`, the pod uses the node's resolver, which can't resolve cluster service names. So every call from the frontend to `cart`, `ad`, `product-catalog`, `checkout`, and so on failed with `Name resolution failed for target dns:...`, and frontend-proxy returned errors for those routes. Revision 1 had `ClusterFirst`, and that was the only spec difference between the two ReplicaSets.\n\n**Change:** I patched the `frontend` deployment in `otel-demo` to `dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst`. The rollout completed, and the new pod runs with that policy.\n\n**Verification:**\n- The new frontend pod shows no resolution errors or HTTP 500s in the last 60 seconds of logs.\n- A test pod resolves `cart.otel-demo.svc.cluster.local` to its ClusterIP.\n- Requests through frontend-proxy to `/`, `/api/products`, `/api/data`, and `/api/cart` all returned `200 OK`.\n- All pods in the namespace are Running.\n\n**Follow-up:** I didn't find out who or what made revision 2. My patch is a live edit, so the next `helm upgrade` of the `otel-demo` release could bring back `Default`. Check the release values and the chart for the frontend's `dnsPolicy` before then.",
    "timed_out": false,
    "turns": 11,
